  "claims": [
   {
    "field": "shape",
    "value": "irregular with pseudopodia",
    "quote": "A macrophage is an irregularly shaped phagocyte that is amoeboid in nature and is the most versatile of the phagocytes in the body. Macrophages move through tissues and squeeze through capillary walls using pseudopodia.",
    "source": {
     "section": "21.2 Barrier Defenses and the Innate Immune Response",
     "url": "https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/21-2-barrier-defenses-and-the-innate-immune-response",
     "license": "CC BY 4.0",
     "citation": "Betts JG, Young KA, Wise JA, Johnson E, Poe B, Kruse DH, Korol O, Johnson JE, Womble M, DeSaix P (2022). Anatomy and Physiology 2e. OpenStax.",
     "access": "Access for free at https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/1-introduction"
    }
   },
   {
    "field": "surface",
    "value": "pseudopodia",
    "quote": "Macrophages move through tissues and squeeze through capillary walls using pseudopodia.",
    "source": {
     "section": "21.2 Barrier Defenses and the Innate Immune Response",
     "url": "https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/21-2-barrier-defenses-and-the-innate-immune-response",
     "license": "CC BY 4.0",
     "citation": "Betts JG, Young KA, Wise JA, Johnson E, Poe B, Kruse DH, Korol O, Johnson JE, Womble M, DeSaix P (2022). Anatomy and Physiology 2e. OpenStax.",
     "access": "Access for free at https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/1-introduction"
    }
   },
   {
    "field": "nucleus_count",
    "value": "1",
    "quote": "A mononuclear phagocyte present in variety of tissues, typically differentiated from monocytes",
    "source": {
     "section": "CL:0000235 macrophage",
     "url": "http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000235",
     "license": "CC BY 4.0",
     "citation": "Cell Ontology (CL), https://obofoundry.org/ontology/cl.html"
    }
   },
   {
    "field": "organelle",
    "value": "lysosomes: 3 abundant",
    "quote": "when certain immune defense cells (white blood cells) phagocytize bacteria, the bacterial cell is transported into a lysosome and digested by the enzymes inside. As one might imagine, such phagocytic defense cells contain large numbers of lysosomes.",
    "source": {
     "section": "3.2 The Cytoplasm and Cellular Organelles",
     "url": "https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-2-the-cytoplasm-and-cellular-organelles",
     "license": "CC BY 4.0",
     "citation": "Betts JG, Young KA, Wise JA, Johnson E, Poe B, Kruse DH, Korol O, Johnson JE, Womble M, DeSaix P (2022). Anatomy and Physiology 2e. OpenStax.",
     "access": "Access for free at https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/1-introduction"
    }
   },
   {
    "field": "special",
    "value": "class II MHC molecules",
    "quote": "Class II | Macrophage | Yes | Stimulates phagocytosis and presentation at primary infection site",
    "source": {
     "section": "21.3 The Adaptive Immune Response: T lymphocytes and Their Functional Types",
     "url": "https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/21-3-the-adaptive-immune-response-t-lymphocytes-and-their-functional-types",
     "license": "CC BY 4.0",
     "citation": "Betts JG, Young KA, Wise JA, Johnson E, Poe B, Kruse DH, Korol O, Johnson JE, Womble M, DeSaix P (2022). Anatomy and Physiology 2e. OpenStax.",
     "access": "Access for free at https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/1-introduction"
    }
   },
   {
    "field": "function",
    "value": "After leaving the blood as a monocyte, it engulfs debris, foreign pathogens, worn-out red blood cells and other dead or damaged cells.",
    "quote": "Macrophages are monocytes that have left the circulation and phagocytize debris, foreign pathogens, worn-out erythrocytes, and many other dead, worn out, or damaged cells.",
    "source": {
     "section": "18.4 Leukocytes and Platelets",
     "url": "https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/18-4-leukocytes-and-platelets",
     "license": "CC BY 4.0",
     "citation": "Betts JG, Young KA, Wise JA, Johnson E, Poe B, Kruse DH, Korol O, Johnson JE, Womble M, DeSaix P (2022). Anatomy and Physiology 2e. OpenStax.",
     "access": "Access for free at https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/1-introduction"
    }
   },
   {
    "field": "function",
    "value": "It folds its plasma membrane around a pathogen to seal it in a vesicle, which fuses with a lysosome whose hydrolytic enzymes destroy the pathogen.",
    "quote": "macrophage's plasma membrane invaginates (folds in) and engulfs a pathogen. The invaginated section, with the pathogen inside, then pinches itself off from the plasma membrane and becomes a vesicle. The vesicle fuses with a lysosome. The lysosome’s hydrolytic enzymes then destroy the pathogen",
    "source": {
     "section": "4.4 The Endomembrane System and Proteins",
     "url": "https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-4-the-endomembrane-system-and-proteins",
     "license": "CC BY 4.0",
     "citation": "Clark MA, Douglas M, Choi J (2018). Biology 2e. OpenStax.",
     "access": "Access for free at https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-introduction"
    }
   },
   {
    "field": "function",
    "value": "Its actin microfilaments bend the plasma membrane around a pathogen, and microtubules then carry the sealed vesicle to a lysosome for digestion.",
    "quote": "A macrophage engulfs a pathogen by rearranging its actin microfilaments to bend the plasma membrane around the pathogen. Once the pathogen is sealed in an endosome inside the macrophage, the vesicle is walked along microtubules until it combines with a lysosome to digest the pathogen.",
    "source": {
     "section": "4.5 The Cytoskeleton",
     "url": "https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-5-the-cytoskeleton",
     "license": "CC BY 4.0",
     "citation": "Clark MA, Douglas M, Choi J (2018). Biology 2e. OpenStax.",
     "access": "Access for free at https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-introduction"
    }
   },
   {
    "field": "function",
    "value": "Free macrophages crawl rapidly by amoeboid movement to engulf infectious agents and cellular debris, while fixed macrophages stay as permanent residents of their tissues.",
    "quote": "Roaming, or free, macrophages move rapidly by amoeboid movement, engulfing infectious agents and cellular debris. In contrast, fixed macrophages are permanent residents of their tissues.",
    "source": {
     "section": "4.3 Connective Tissue Supports and Protects",
     "url": "https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/4-3-connective-tissue-supports-and-protects",
     "license": "CC BY 4.0",
     "citation": "Betts JG, Young KA, Wise JA, Johnson E, Poe B, Kruse DH, Korol O, Johnson JE, Womble M, DeSaix P (2022). Anatomy and Physiology 2e. OpenStax.",
     "access": "Access for free at https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/1-introduction"
    }
   },
   {
    "field": "function",
    "value": "When stimulated it secretes cytokines, small protein messengers that recruit other immune cells to infected sites and stimulate their activities.",
    "quote": "When stimulated, macrophages release cytokines, small proteins that act as chemical messengers. Cytokines recruit other cells of the immune system to infected sites and stimulate their activities.",
    "source": {
     "section": "4.3 Connective Tissue Supports and Protects",
     "url": "https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/4-3-connective-tissue-supports-and-protects",
     "license": "CC BY 4.0",
     "citation": "Betts JG, Young KA, Wise JA, Johnson E, Poe B, Kruse DH, Korol O, Johnson JE, Womble M, DeSaix P (2022). Anatomy and Physiology 2e. OpenStax.",
     "access": "Access for free at https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/1-introduction"
    }
   },
   {
    "field": "function",
    "value": "It releases antimicrobial defensins and chemotactic chemicals that draw other white blood cells to an infection site.",
    "quote": "Macrophages also release antimicrobial defensins and chemotactic chemicals that attract other leukocytes to the site of an infection.",
    "source": {
     "section": "18.4 Leukocytes and Platelets",
     "url": "https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/18-4-leukocytes-and-platelets",
     "license": "CC BY 4.0",
     "citation": "Betts JG, Young KA, Wise JA, Johnson E, Poe B, Kruse DH, Korol O, Johnson JE, Womble M, DeSaix P (2022). Anatomy and Physiology 2e. OpenStax.",
     "access": "Access for free at https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/1-introduction"
    }
   },
   {
    "field": "function",
    "value": "Carrying class II MHC molecules, it is a phagocytic antigen-presenting cell that stimulates phagocytosis and antigen presentation at the primary infection site.",
    "quote": "Class II | Macrophage | Yes | Stimulates phagocytosis and presentation at primary infection site",
    "source": {
     "section": "21.3 The Adaptive Immune Response: T lymphocytes and Their Functional Types",
     "url": "https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/21-3-the-adaptive-immune-response-t-lymphocytes-and-their-functional-types",
     "license": "CC BY 4.0",
     "citation": "Betts JG, Young KA, Wise JA, Johnson E, Poe B, Kruse DH, Korol O, Johnson JE, Womble M, DeSaix P (2022). Anatomy and Physiology 2e. OpenStax.",
     "access": "Access for free at https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/1-introduction"
    }
   },
   {
    "field": "function",
    "value": "Its oxidative metabolism, often producing nitric oxide, kills bacteria that survive inside the cell.",
    "quote": "Macrophage oxidative metabolism is hostile to intracellular bacteria, often relying on the production of nitric oxide to kill the bacteria inside the macrophage.",
    "source": {
     "section": "21.5 The Immune Response against Pathogens",
     "url": "https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/21-5-the-immune-response-against-pathogens",
     "license": "CC BY 4.0",
     "citation": "Betts JG, Young KA, Wise JA, Johnson E, Poe B, Kruse DH, Korol O, Johnson JE, Womble M, DeSaix P (2022). Anatomy and Physiology 2e. OpenStax.",
     "access": "Access for free at https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/1-introduction"
    }
   },
   {
    "field": "function",
    "value": "It continuously makes complement proteins, part of a system of soluble proteins that destroys extracellular pathogens.",
    "quote": "An array of approximately 20 types of soluble proteins, called a complement system, functions to destroy extracellular pathogens. Cells of the liver and macrophages synthesize complement proteins continuously",
    "source": {
     "section": "42.1 Innate Immune Response",
     "url": "https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/42-1-innate-immune-response",
     "license": "CC BY 4.0",
     "citation": "Clark MA, Douglas M, Choi J (2018). Biology 2e. OpenStax.",
     "access": "Access for free at https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-introduction"
    }
   }
  ]
 },
